API Gateway Governance Document  
 

1. Purpose and Scope  

This Governance document establishes the minimum requirements and organizational guardrails for the design, deployment, security, and operation of API gateway platforms within the enterprise. 

The intent is to ensure secure access, consistent design patterns, centralized visibility, reliable operations, and cost‑responsible API usage. 

This Governance applies to all API gateways supporting REST, HTTP, event‑driven, or hybrid API patterns across all environments. 

 

2. API Design and Development  

API Definitions: All APIs must be defined using structured, machine‑readable specifications.  

Naming Conventions: API names should follow a standard format. 

Version Control: Versioning must be explicit and applied consistently to prevent breaking changes. 

Documentation: API documentation must be published and accessible for consumers.  

Payload Compression: Payload compression and performance‑optimized configurations must be used when appropriate. 

 

3. Security and Access Control  

All APIs must enforce enterprise-approved authentication and authorization mechanisms  

All external-facing APIs must be protected by centralized threat protection controls  

All API traffic must be encrypted in transit using enterprise-approved encryption standards  

Sensitive data exposed via APIs must comply with enterprise data classification and protection policies 

 

4. Identity and Access Management 

API access must be governed through centralized identity providers 

Least privilege access must be enforced for all API consumers and operators 

Machine-to-machine access must use approved service authentication patterns 

 

5. Network and Exposure Controls 

Internal APIs must not be publicly exposed 

APIs must be deployed within approved network boundaries 

External exposure must follow enterprise-defined ingress controls and review processes 

 

6. Deployment and Change Governance 

All API gateway configurations must be deployed using approved Infrastructure as Code mechanisms 

All production changes must undergo change management and approval processes 

Unauthorized modifications to production APIs must be preventable and detectable 

 

7. Observability and Operations 

APIs must emit centralized logs, metrics, and traces 

Monitoring must detect:  

Failures 

Latency degradation 

Traffic anomalies 

Support ownership must be clearly defined for every API 

 

8. Data Protection and Resilience 

APIs must not expose sensitive data without appropriate controls 

API platforms must support data durability and recovery capabilities 

Resilience mechanisms must be implemented to prevent cascading failures 

 

9. Financial Governance 

API usage must be metered and attributable to a defined cost owner 

Cost controls must be implemented to prevent excessive or abusive usage 

APIs must be tagged with ownership and cost allocation metadata 

 

10. Compliance and Audit 

All API configuration and access changes must be auditable 

APIs must undergo security and architecture review prior to production release 

Periodic compliance reviews must be conducted 

 

 

 

 
